{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 21, :comments ["<b>Who knows.</b> It is known that the definite hei, if one of the letters alef, het, ayin, resh follows it, has a kamatz gadol in the majority, while that of the interrogative is always with a patach, and the ascent of the hei upward is a kamatz gadol. Also there is a dagesh in the yod after the definite hei, as in היושב, whereas if it were interrogative there would be shva and patach under the hei, and the yod rafeh. The meaning: who and who among the children of men knows the difference that exists between the spirit of man and the spirit of the beast? The matter is that not one of a thousand will be found, and the knowledge of the spirit is deep and requires proof. They cannot understand even a part of it except the maskilim whose thought has been clarified in the balances of wisdom and in its four foundations, three of which are number, counted, and counting, and one composed of the two. And from the Torah of our Elohim one can understand, for He said concerning the kinds of plants: 'Let the earth bring forth grass' — and behold, the earth has power to cause growth by the word of Elohim. And because that which grows is a body, from it they came forth and to it they return, and that which the earth brought forth does not endure except for days fixed, because there is a limit to its growth; and when it stands and grows no more, it returns backward. Likewise He said: 'Let the waters swarm with living soul' — and behold, the waters brought forth the soul of every land-creature, bird, and fish, by the word of Elohim. And likewise He said: 'Let the earth bring forth a living soul after its kind' — but He did not say in the Torah 'the soul of man'; rather He said: 'Let us make man in our image, after our likeness.' And He mentioned that He created his body from the earth, and afterward: 'and He breathed into his nostrils the breath of life' — and He hinted 'life' because it endures and does not perish, unlike the soul of the beast. And there is a difference between neshamah and nefesh, for we find not in all of Scripture neshamah except upon the children of men, as in 'who gives neshamah to the people upon it,' and 'all in whose nostrils is the breath of the spirit of life' — this returns upon man alone. And Solomon said at the end of the book that the spirit of the children of men returns to Elohim, and not to the dust."], :book :ecclesiastes, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 3, :notes {0 "The phrase 'ספר וספר וספור' is rendered literally as 'number, counted, and counting'; the meaning of these three among Ibn Ezra's 'four foundations of wisdom' is a technical matter he treats elsewhere and is left untranslated without expansion. The phrase 'מורכב מן השנים' is rendered 'composed of the two,' an opaque reference left as-is."}, :source-witnesses [["Wikisource" "https://he.wikisource.org/wiki/%D7%90%D7%91%D7%9F_%D7%A2%D7%96%D7%A8%D7%90_%D7%A2%D7%9C_%D7%A7%D7%94%D7%9C%D7%AA"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:27:31.424442436Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}